.TH WK 1 "2026-10-23" "wk" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wk \- manage Git worktrees and their development sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wk
.RI [ --herdr | --tmux | --backend " backend" ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wk
manages linked worktrees in bare Git repositories and traditional clones.
It starts and manages a Herdr workspace by default, or a tmux session when
configured or requested.
.PP
Run commands from the project root, any linked worktree, or their
subdirectories.  Git's common repository metadata identifies the owning
project and its layout automatically; no layout flag is needed.
Bare projects create worktrees directly under the project root, normally
next to the
.I .bare
Git directory.  Traditional clones create them under
.I .worktrees/
in the main checkout.  Both layouts use the same sanitized branch names.
Existing registered worktrees are not relocated.
.PP
The owning project root contains
.IR .wk.toml .
In traditional clones the main checkout's config is authoritative, even when
linked checkouts contain tracked copies.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI add " branch-name [badge]"
Create a worktree for a new branch from the caller's current HEAD in a
traditional clone, or from the configured base ref in a bare repository.
The caller's checkout is used even from a linked worktree or subdirectory;
a detached HEAD is supported.  Uncommitted changes are not copied.
An existing local branch is reused.  Otherwise, if
.I origin/branch-name
exists, check it out and track it instead.
.TP
.BI add " origin/branch-name [badge]"
Create a worktree with a local branch named
.I branch-name
that tracks the specified remote branch.
.PP
An optional badge prefixes the Herdr workspace label with
.BR [badge] ,
leaving the configured label (including its project suffix) after it.
Supplying a badge also updates an existing workspace's label.  Omitting it
leaves an existing label unchanged.  Branch names and worktree paths do not
change.  Badges are stored only in Herdr's label; supply the badge again when
recreating a closed workspace.  Badges require the Herdr backend.
.PP
Setup runs in the launching pane; keep that pane open, but you may switch to
another pane, tab, or workspace while it runs.  Herdr workspace creation uses
the explicit project root as its source, rather than the focused pane.
Inside Herdr, add focuses the destination only if the launching workspace,
tab, and pane are still focused when it finishes.  If you switch elsewhere,
it leaves focus unchanged and reports that the workspace is ready.  Missing
caller identity or an unavailable or incomplete focus snapshot also prevents
automatic switching.  Attaching from outside Herdr and tmux behavior are
unchanged.
.PP
Inside Herdr, add and delete report a temporary working agent named wk in the
invoking pane by default, including during setup and teardown.  The busy
indicator belongs to the invoking workspace, not the destination worktree.
Panes that already have an agent are left alone.  Progress reporting is
best-effort and does not fail the command.  The temporary agent is released on
completion or errors, before attaching or closing a workspace.
Set
.B herdr_progress = false
in the global configuration to opt out.  The same field in
.I .wk.toml
can override the global value with either true or false.
.TP
.BI delete " worktree-dir"
Remove a worktree and its session, delete its branch, and prune stale
worktree metadata.
.I rm
is an alias.  The argument may be a worktree directory, branch name, or
.B .
for the current worktree, including when invoked from a subdirectory.
Traditional clones also accept the sanitized directory name or a
project-relative path such as
.IR .worktrees/feat-login .
Absolute registered-worktree paths and rendered session-template names
(the latter as a compatibility fallback) are also accepted.
Only registered checkouts may be removed.  The main checkout cannot be
deleted, regardless of its branch name.  A Git removal error stops deletion;
locked worktrees are not recursively deleted as a fallback.
.TP
.B clean
Offer to remove worktrees whose pull request has merged.  This command uses
.BR gh (1)
to look up pull-request state.  The main checkout of a traditional clone is
never a cleanup candidate.
.TP
.B ls
Show the project's worktrees and session status, including the main checkout
of a traditional clone.
.TP
.B ls --porcelain
Print parse-friendly three-line blocks: branch, directory, and session.
.TP
.B init
Create a starter
.I .wk.toml
at the owning project root, or in the current directory outside Git.
Refuse to overwrite an existing config.
.TP
.BI completion " zsh|bash|fish"
Print the shell completion script for the selected shell.
.TP
.BR version , " -v" , " --version"
Print the wk version.
.TP
.BR help , " -h" , " --help"
Print command help.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --herdr
Use the Herdr session backend for this invocation.  This is the default unless
configured otherwise.
.TP
.B --tmux
Use the tmux session backend for this invocation.
.TP
.BI --backend " herdr|tmux"
Equivalent to the matching short backend option.  Backend options may appear
before or after the command; if more than one is supplied, the last one wins.
.SH LAYOUT AND SETUP
Before creating a linked worktree in a traditional clone,
.B add
ensures the anchored entry
.B /.worktrees/
exists in the main checkout's
.IR .gitignore .
Existing content is preserved and the entry is not duplicated.  Read or write
errors, or conflicting rules that leave the container unignored, stop creation.
This may leave a tracked ignore file modified; commit the rule to share it
with collaborators.
.PP
Setup and teardown commands run inside the target worktree.  Relative paths
to shared files at the project root therefore use
.I ../
for bare layouts and
.I ../../
for traditional clones.  Shell commands are not rewritten automatically.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .wk.toml
Per-project configuration.  Its optional fields are
.BR name ,
.BR base_ref ,
.BR herdr_progress ,
.BR setup ,
and
.BR teardown .
The base_ref field selects the starting point for new branches only in bare
repositories; traditional clones use the caller's current HEAD.
.TP
.I ~/.config/wk/config.toml
Global configuration.  The supported fields are
.BR default_base_ref ,
.BR session_template ,
.BR session_backend ,
and
.BR herdr_progress
(default true).
The default_base_ref field is the fallback for bare repositories without a
project base_ref; it does not override a traditional clone's current HEAD.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B wk
requires Git.  It requires Herdr for the default backend or tmux when that
backend is selected.  The
.B clean
command additionally needs the GitHub CLI,
.BR gh (1).
Commands in a project's
.B setup
or
.B teardown
configuration are run by
.BR sh (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B wk
exits with status 0 on success and non-zero if a command fails or its arguments
are invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR gh (1)
